<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tiridates I of Armenia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tiridates I was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in the mid-1st century CE and founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsacid-dynasty">Arsacid dynasty</a> of Armenia. A brother of the Parthian king Vologases I, he was placed on the Armenian throne by his brother, provoking war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Under the peace that followed, he travelled to Rome and was crowned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> in 66, so that both empires claimed a stake in Armenia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tiridates-i-of-armenia.jpg" alt="Tiridates I of Armenia" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Panos Terlemezyan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D5%8F%D6%80%D5%A4%D5%A1%D5%BF_%D4%B1_%D5%A9%D5%A1%D5%A3%D5%A1%D5%BE%D5%B8%D6%80._%D5%A8%D5%B6%D5%A4%D6%85%D6%80%D5%AB%D5%B6%D5%A1%D5%AF%D5%B8%D6%82%D5%A9%D5%B5%D5%B8%D6%82%D5%B6_%D6%84%D5%A1%D5%B6%D5%A4%D5%A1%D5%AF%D5%AB%D6%81.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tiridates was the youngest son of Vonones II, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/media-atropatene">Media Atropatene</a> and later of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>, and had two elder brothers, Pacorus and Vologases. His name means given by Tir, a Zoroastrian divinity equated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>. He was also a Zoroastrian priest. When Vonones II died, Vologases I succeeded him, gave Media Atropatene to Pacorus and invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, where the Iberian prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhadamistus">Rhadamistus</a> had seized the throne after killing his uncle Mithridates, the legitimate king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Vologases took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxata">Artaxata</a> and proclaimed Tiridates king. A winter epidemic and a revolt by his son Vardanes forced him to withdraw, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, punished by the returning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhadamistus">Rhadamistus</a>, revolted and replaced him with Tiridates. Nero sent the general Gnaeus Domitius Corbulo east, and in the spring of 58 Corbulo entered Greater Armenia, burned Artaxata and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranocerta">Tigranocerta</a>. Tiridates fled, and Nero gave the crown to a prince who took the name Tigranes. After the Roman commander Lucius Caesennius Paetus suffered a defeat at Rhandeia in 62, Corbulo and Tiridates met there to agree peace. Tiridates laid his diadem near a statue of Nero, agreeing to receive it back only from the emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><p>He travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 66 with his wife, children, brothers, 3,000 Parthian horsemen and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. The journey took nine months and went overland, since he did not wish to pollute water, one of the seven Zoroastrian holy elements. In the Forum he knelt and told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> he would be whatever the emperor ordered, and Nero set the diadem on his head. Nero gave him fifty million sesterces. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> he brought artisans to rebuild <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxata">Artaxata</a>, renamed Neronia, and embellished the royal residence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-garni">Garni</a>. In 72 Alans raided northern Armenia, and he narrowly escaped capture when he was lassoed and cut the rope with his sword.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Under the settlement the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> was to be a Parthian prince, but his appointment required Roman approval; some Roman sources thought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> had in effect ceded Armenia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>. The peace between the two empires lasted 50 years, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> invaded Armenia in 114. Tiridates began reforming Armenia's administration, bringing in many Iranian customs and offices. The date of his death is unknown, and various sources name Sanatruk as his successor. It has been suggested that his visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> lies behind the Christian story of the Three Magi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tiridates I of Armenia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Arsacid branch took hold when <b>Tiridates I</b>, brother of the Parthian king, was crowned by Nero in Rome in 66 CE, a compromise that followed the Roman defeat at Rhandeia and let both empires claim a stake.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-armenia-arsacid">Kingdom of Armenia (Arsacid)</a></li></ul></section>
